## Target version and changelog

Inspect the project context and repository documentation to identify the project's versioning and changelog conventions. Compare the current branch's version with the primary branch without checking out or modifying that branch when the repository and documented conventions make this possible. If the project has no version or changelog convention, do not invent one; recommend skipping that action.

Ask only one question at a time. First ask which target version, if any, the user wants. Base the recommendation on the accepted user-visible changes, existing unmerged version work, and the project's documented conventions. Explain ambiguity rather than guessing. Do not modify version or changelog files until the user answers. If the user requests any other feature or documentation change at any point in wrap-up, stop release work, set `decision` to `changes_requested`, choose the latest appropriate return step, and report the request precisely.

After the user confirms the target, update the version and changelog only when required by the project's conventions. Write concise, user-centered release notes describing capabilities and visible compatibility changes rather than implementation details. Preserve existing notes for an already-unmerged target.

## Commit and pull request

Inspect the current repository state and determine whether a pull request for the current branch already exists, including non-open states when the available tooling supports that lookup. Check existing pull requests before proposing or attempting PR creation. If one exists, retrieve and report its URL and do not create a duplicate. Otherwise, propose creating a PR.

Prepare one concise release plan covering the exact commit scope and message, any required push, and whether an existing PR will be used or a new PR created. Ask the user one question to approve or revise that complete plan. Perform only the approved plan, in a safe order. When authorized to create a PR, check once more whether one now exists; use its URL if it does, otherwise create the PR and report the resulting URL.

Do not include unrelated working-tree changes in a commit. Never overwrite user work, rewrite history, force-push, or publish anything beyond the approved plan. Set `decision` to `complete` only after reporting exactly what was done, skipped, or left for the user, the PR URL when one exists, and the final repository state.
